.TH CPIOFS 4
.SH NAME
cpiofs \- mount cpio archive
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cpiofs
[
.B -ab
] [
.B -p
]
.I cpiofile
.I mountpt
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Cpiofs
makes the contents of the directories and files archived in the given
.I cpio
archive
.I cpiofile
visible in the name space under
.IR mountpt .
They can then be accessed in an obvious way by other commands
such as
.IR ls (1)
and
.IR cp (1).
Only read access is supported.
.PP
Three portable
.I cpio
formats are recognised, distinguished by their leading six-character
magic strings:
.TP 12
.B 070701
the SVR4 portable (``newc'') format with ASCII hex headers
.TP
.B 070702
the SVR4 portable format with CRC, treated identically (the CRC field is
not verified)
.TP
.B 070707
the old POSIX portable (``odc'') format with ASCII octal headers
.PD
.PP
The older binary cpio formats are not supported because they are
byte-order dependent and have not been produced by standard tools
since the late 1980s.
.PP
User and group IDs are not preserved; all entries are reported as
owned by uid/gid
.BR 0 .
Symbolic links present in the archive are silently skipped: only
regular files and directories appear in the mounted view.
.PP
The
.B -a
and
.B -b
options cause the
.IR cpiofile 's
name space to be placed after or before the current contents of
.IR mountpt ;
the default is to replace that directory's contents.
Option
.B -p
causes permissions in the cpio file to be discarded, making all
entries read-only.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/cpiofs.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR tarfs (4),
.IR archfs (4),
.IR intro (5)
